  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is drospirenone, a rigid lipophilic 17-spirolactone steroid; no drospirenone-specific F record is present, but the closest L4 analog spironolactone, sharing the 17-spirolactone/3-keto-4-ene core, has an experimental human fraction absorbed of 100% (C112), and canrenone, the spirolactone metabolite, produced substantial oral AUC in humans (C68, C56, C70, C82).",
      "card_ids": ["C112", "C68", "C56", "C70", "C82"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 consistently reports high human fraction absorbed for lipophilic steroids: dexamethasone 100% (C108), prednisolone 95% (C134), hydrocortisone 89–95% (C122, C152), corticosterone 100% (C145), testosterone 100% (C146, C113), progesterone 91% (C153), prednisone >95% (C155), and methylprednisolone fa 0.82 (C107).",
      "card_ids": ["C108", "C134", "C122", "C152", "C145", "C146", "C113", "C153", "C155", "C107"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 adds mechanistic gut-wall evidence: budesonide showed high bidirectional permeability with efflux ratio 1 and no P-gp efflux (C199, C157, C188, C176, C178); testosterone and hydrocortisone are high-permeability passive-transport references (C187, C191, C196, C183); progesterone transport was enhanced across Caco-2/everted sac (C198); prednisone showed rapid uptake in gut-chip (C184). These support high intestinal absorption without efflux limitation for lipophilic steroids.",
      "card_ids": ["C199", "C157", "C188", "C176", "C178", "C187", "C191", "C196", "C183", "C198", "C184"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Some L5 records show P-gp efflux for other steroids (prednisolone C194, estrone C156, ethynyl estradiol C166), but these are structurally less similar to drospirenone (corticosteroid/phenolic estrogens) and do not provide affirmative drospirenone-specific presystemic loss; mechanism alone is not an F result.",
      "card_ids": ["C194", "C156", "C166"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F<20%; the unfavorable solubility prior is not an F measurement, and L3/L4/L5 absorption/permeability evidence supports the absorption component.",
      "card_ids": ["C112", "C108", "C199"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C199", "C157", "C188", "C176", "C178", "C187", "C191", "C196", "C183", "C198", "C184", "C185", "C197", "C175", "C203", "C172", "C160", "C170", "C163"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No drospirenone-specific absolute oral bioavailability record is present in any level; all F evidence comes from reference contexts and structural analogs.",
    "No drospirenone-specific gut-wall efflux or intestinal metabolism record is present in L5; the records are for other steroids/compounds.",
    "L5 efflux records for prednisolone, estrone, and ethynyl estradiol are low-transferability and do not establish drospirenone presystemic loss.",
    "Query species, formulation, dose, prandial state, and disease condition are unspecified; species-dependent F remains possible.",
    "No drospirenone-specific solubility or dissolution measurement is available; the unfavorable solubility prior is not affirmative evidence of F<20%.",
    "L3 oral AUC records lack IV comparators and measure canrenone or co-administered hormones, not drospirenone itself."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L5 adds mechanistic gut-wall evidence that lipophilic steroids generally show high passive permeability and, for budesonide/testosterone/hydrocortisone/progesterone, no efflux limitation; the few P-gp substrate records involve structurally distant steroids and are low-transferability. Combined with L4 spironolactone 100% fraction absorbed and L3 canrenone oral exposure, F>=20% remains better supported; prediction high, moderate confidence, strengthened."
}

